---
import Layout from "../layouts/BlogPost.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About Ben Brannen"
  description="Ben Brannen builds practical intelligence tools for dashboards, market research, public records, automation, and Small Town Capital."
  pubDate={new Date("June 3 2026")}
  heroImage="/atl_thon.jpg"
>
  <style>
    :global(.prose.prose) {
      width: min(920px, calc(100% - 2rem)) !important;
      max-width: 920px !important;
      margin-inline: auto !important;
      padding-inline: 0 !important;
    }

    :global(.title.title) {
      max-width: none !important;
    }

    .about-lede {
      color: rgb(var(--ink-muted));
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .about-card {
      background:
        linear-gradient(145deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.105), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.035)),
        var(--glass-bg);
      backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(125%);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(125%);
      border: 1px solid var(--glass-border);
      border-radius: 18px;
      box-shadow: var(--glass-shadow);
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
      padding: 1.55rem 1.7rem;
    }

    .about-card h2,
    .about-card h3 {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .theme-panel {
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
      padding: 1.55rem 1.7rem;
    }

    .theme-panel > p {
      color: rgb(var(--ink-muted));
      margin-top: -0.25rem;
    }

    .theme-list {
      display: grid;
      gap: 1rem;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      list-style: none;
      margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    .theme-list li {
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.055);
      border: 1px solid rgba(238, 243, 246, 0.12);
      border-radius: 14px;
      padding: 0.95rem;
    }

    .theme-list strong {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    }

    .pill-list {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0.55rem;
      list-style: none;
      margin: 1rem 0 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    .pill-list li {
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.055);
      border: 1px solid rgba(238, 243, 246, 0.12);
      border-radius: 999px;
      color: rgb(var(--ink));
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      line-height: 1.25;
      padding: 0.45rem 0.72rem;
    }

    .small-note {
      color: rgb(var(--ink-muted));
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    .connect-box {
      background:
        linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(200, 155, 95, 0.12), rgba(55, 80, 92, 0.16)),
        var(--glass-bg);
      backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(125%);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(125%);
      border: 1px solid rgba(238, 243, 246, 0.14);
      border-radius: 20px;
      margin-top: 2rem;
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }

    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .about-lede {
        font-size: 1.12rem;
      }

      .theme-list {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <p class="about-lede">
    I’m Ben. I live in Waynesboro, Georgia, and I build practical tools around
    messy systems: dashboards, market notes, public records, automation, and
    small experiments that usually start because I wanted to understand something
    better.
  </p>

  <div class="about-card">
    <h2>What I’m building now</h2>
    <p>
      Most of the current work sits under the Small Town Capital idea: a private
      market research dashboard, options notes, public-record projects, automation
      workflows, and this site as the public notebook.
    </p>
    <p>
      I use AI coding tools heavily, but the point is not to look automated. The
      point is to build faster, keep better receipts, and still know what has to
      be checked by hand.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="about-card theme-panel">
    <h2>Things I keep coming back to</h2>
    <p>
      The common thread is taking scattered information and turning it into
      cleaner decisions, repeatable workflows, and a public trail of the work.
    </p>
    <ul class="theme-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Dashboards</strong>
        Taking information that lives in ten scattered places and putting it in
        one place I can actually use.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Automation</strong>
        Scripts, spreadsheets, APIs, and agents that remove repetitive work.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Investing</strong>
        Researching companies, options structures, and trade decisions for my own
        account, then writing down what I learn, especially the mistakes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public records</strong>
        Filings, meeting minutes, property records, and datasets that are public
        but annoying to use, plus tools that make the trail easier to follow.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="about-card">
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>
      Mostly to write things down. Projects make more sense to me after I explain
      them, and half-finished experiments are easier to pick back up when there
      are notes. If I cannot describe a project plainly, it probably is not as far
      along as I think.
    </p>
    <p>
      The public version is an operator journal: what I saw, what I built, what
      broke, and what became a rule for next time. The private work stays private.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="about-card">
    <h2>Tools and background</h2>
    <p>
      I studied finance at Georgia Southern University and have spent years
      working across spreadsheets, data visualization, automation, scripting,
      and practical problem solving.
    </p>
    <ul class="pill-list" aria-label="Technical skills and tools">
      <li>Google Sheets</li>
      <li>Excel</li>
      <li>Apps Script</li>
      <li>Python</li>
      <li>SQL</li>
      <li>Bash / Linux</li>
      <li>Looker Studio</li>
      <li>Power BI</li>
      <li>Astro</li>
      <li>HTML / CSS / JavaScript</li>
      <li>Raspberry Pi</li>
      <li>Google Cloud Certified Digital Leader</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="about-card">
    <h2>Outside of projects</h2>
    <p>
      I’m also a runner, a skier, a self-taught guitar player, and someone who
      has a habit of turning personal challenges into data projects.
    </p>
    <p>
      One example: I once ran <a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com/u/0/reporting/29998d2b-7d6e-4f42-b4e7-cfa489bff4a9/page/tlXZD">102 straight days of 5K or more</a>
      and built a dashboard to keep score. Do the thing, track the thing, learn
      from the thing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="connect-box">
    <h2>Say hello</h2>
    <p>
      If you want to talk about dashboards, automation, public records, markets,
      running, or a project you are building, reach out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Email: <a href="mailto:benjamin.m.brannen@gmail.com">benjamin.m.brannen@gmail.com</a><br />
      LinkedIn: <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/benjamin-brannen">linkedin.com/in/benjamin-brannen</a>
    </p>
    <p class="small-note">
      Nothing here is investment advice. This site is a public notebook for
      projects, experiments, research habits, and lessons learned while building.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
